The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
q->ndesc = ndesc;
```
```
q->page_pool = page_pool_create(&pp_params);
```
```
q->desc = dmam_alloc_coherent(eth->dev, q->ndesc * sizeof(*q->desc),
			      &dma_addr, GFP_KERNEL);
```
Reason:
The early assignment `q->ndesc = ndesc` sets `ndesc` to a non‑zero value before any memory allocation or `netif_napi_add()`. If the subsequent `devm_kzalloc` for `q->entry` or `page_pool_create` or `dmam_alloc_coherent` fails, `airoha_qdma_cleanup()` will check `q->ndesc` (which is non‑zero) and attempt `netif_napi_del()` on a NAPI that was never added, causing a NULL pointer dereference. Additionally, allocating `page_pool` before the DMA descriptor list can lead to a memory leak if the DMA allocation fails.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
+	q->desc = dmam_alloc_coherent(eth->dev, ndesc * sizeof(*q->desc),
+				      &dma_addr, GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!q->desc)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
 	q->page_pool = page_pool_create(&pp_params);
 	if (IS_ERR(q->page_pool)) {
 		int err = PTR_ERR(q->page_pool);

 		q->page_pool = NULL;
 		return err;
 	}

-	q->desc = dmam_alloc_coherent(eth->dev, q->ndesc * sizeof(*q->desc),
-				      &dma_addr, GFP_KERNEL);
-	if (!q->desc)
-		return -ENOMEM;
-
+	q->ndesc = ndesc;
```
Reason:
By moving `q->ndesc = ndesc` to the end of the function, after all allocations and after `netif_napi_add()`, any failure in `dmam_alloc_coherent` or `page_pool_create` will leave `q->ndesc` at its initial value (zero). Consequently, `airoha_qdma_cleanup()` will not call `netif_napi_del()` on a nonexistent NAPI, preventing the NULL pointer dereference. Also, the DMA descriptor allocation is now performed before `page_pool_create`, so if the descriptor allocation fails, the page pool is never allocated, avoiding a memory leak.

